        <h1 class="font-serif font-black text-white text-4xl sm:text-5xl leading-tight">Sigiriya vs Pidurangala:<br/>Which Rock Should You Climb?</h1>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-4 text-white/70 text-sm mt-4">
          <span>June 2025</span><span>·</span><span>7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-a:text-ceylon-green-700">

        <p>Two granite inselbergs rise from the flat jungle of Sri Lanka's Cultural Triangle, 200 metres apart and about 200 million years old. One carries a 5th-century palace complex, a UNESCO World Heritage designation and an entry fee of USD 35. The other has a reclining Buddha at its base, a boulder scramble to the summit, and costs USD 3.</p>

        <p>Thousands of travellers ask this question every day: <em>which one should I climb?</em> The honest answer is that it depends on what you want from the experience — but the best answer, if you have a morning to spare, is both.</p>

        <h2>The Case for Sigiriya (USD 35)</h2>

        <p><a href="/places/sigiriya">Sigiriya</a> is not merely a rock to climb. It is one of the ancient world's most extraordinary building projects — a palace complex constructed on top of, inside and around a 200-metre vertical granite plug by King Kashyapa between 477 and 495 AD. The engineering is staggering. The artistic achievement is rare. To dismiss it because of the entry fee is to miss something genuinely irreplaceable.</p>

        <p><strong>What makes Sigiriya worth USD 35:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The Lion Paws</strong> — at the base of the final climb, two enormous stone lion paws are all that remain of a colossal lion gateway through which visitors once passed. The scale is humbling.</li>
          <li><strong>The Cloud Maidens frescoes</strong> — halfway up the rock, a sheltered gallery holds ancient paintings of celestial women (apsaras) in styles comparable to Ajanta in India. Dating from the 5th century, they are among the finest ancient paintings in Asia. Photographs cannot capture the quality of the pigments up close.</li>
          <li><strong>The Mirror Wall</strong> — polished originally to a reflective surface, the wall beside the frescoes gallery carries 1,500 years of carved visitor graffiti, some dating to the 8th century. Ancient Sri Lankan poetry, love notes and commentary on the frescoes.</li>
          <li><strong>The water gardens</strong> — the grounds around the base contain sophisticated hydraulic gardens with fountains that still partially function on the same ancient engineering. Worth 30 minutes before you begin climbing.</li>
          <li><strong>360° summit views</strong> — from the top, you see the entire Cultural Triangle spread out in every direction: jungle, tanks, distant mountains. There is no equivalent view anywhere nearby.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Case for Pidurangala (USD 3)</h2>

        <p><a href="/places/pidurangala">Pidurangala</a> is the rock that lets you see Sigiriya properly. The famous image of Lion Rock rising above the jungle canopy — the one on every travel magazine, every Instagram post — is taken from Pidurangala's summit. You cannot get that photograph from Sigiriya itself.</p>

        <p><strong>What makes Pidurangala worth climbing:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The iconic view</strong> — Sigiriya framed by jungle, sky and the surrounding Cultural Triangle, with the rock rising as it was meant to be seen: from a distance, in its proper context. This is the best single viewpoint in the Cultural Triangle.</li>
          <li><strong>Sunrise</strong> — arriving at Pidurangala at 5am (it opens at 5am, entry LKR 300/USD 1 at this hour) and climbing in the dark with a torch, to sit on the summit boulders as the sun rises behind you and lights up Sigiriya — this is genuinely one of Sri Lanka's great experiences. There are usually only a dozen or two people at sunrise even in peak season.</li>
          <li><strong>The boulder scramble</strong> — the final section of Pidurangala requires clambering over large boulders with your hands. It is physically more demanding than Sigiriya's staircase but far more exciting. No handrails. No crowds pushing from behind.</li>
          <li><strong>The cave temple</strong> — at the base of Pidurangala, a 5th-century cave temple contains a 10-metre reclining Buddha carved directly into the rock. Ancient, atmospheric and included in the entry fee. Visit before or after the climb.</li>
          <li><strong>USD 3</strong> — one-twelfth the price of Sigiriya. The best value-for-money viewpoint in Sri Lanka.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>

