        <h2>The Honest Verdict</h2>

        <p><strong>If you can only do one:</strong> Do <a href="/places/sigiriya">Sigiriya</a>. The history, the frescoes, the engineering and the summit views are irreplaceable. Pidurangala without Sigiriya is a beautiful rock with a view of a rock you haven't climbed — you miss the context.</p>

        <p><strong>If you're a photographer:</strong> Pidurangala is non-negotiable. The iconic Sigiriya photograph cannot be taken from Sigiriya. Period.</p>

        <p><strong>If you're budget-conscious:</strong> <a href="/places/pidurangala">Pidurangala</a> at USD 3 is one of the best-value experiences in Sri Lanka. Pair it with visiting Sigiriya's water gardens and lion paws from the outside (free), and you get 80% of the experience for 8% of the price.</p>

        <p><strong>The best plan — do both in one morning:</strong></p>
        <ol>
          <li>Arrive at Pidurangala at <strong>5:00 am</strong> (torch required). Climb in the dark, reach the summit by 5:45am.</li>
          <li>Watch sunrise from the summit boulders. Photograph Sigiriya as it lights up. Descend by 7:15am.</li>
          <li>Walk or tuk-tuk to Sigiriya (200 metres as the crow flies, 800 metres by road). Gates open at 7am.</li>
          <li>Climb Sigiriya in the relative cool of the early morning, before crowds build after 9am.</li>
          <li>Finish by 11am before the heat becomes punishing.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>The total cost: USD 38 for both. The total experience: two completely different perspectives on the same landscape, one ancient and archaeological, one wild and photographic. Most people who do both say it's the best morning of their Sri Lanka trip.</p>

